  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "Why does the coil spin in a motor?",
      "a": "Current in the coil makes it a magnet. Its poles are pushed and pulled by the fixed magnets around it, one side up and the other down, so the coil turns.",
      "asks": [
        "What makes a motor turn?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the commutator do?",
      "a": "Every half turn, the commutator swaps the connections to the coil and reverses its current. That flips the coil's magnetism just in time, so it keeps turning the same way instead of rocking back.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a motor keep spinning the same way?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the shaft of a motor do?",
      "a": "The coil is fixed to the shaft, so the shaft spins with it. Gears, wheels, fan blades or a drill bit are attached to the shaft to use the turning force.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a motor drive things?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How can you make a motor spin faster or stronger?",
      "a": "Use a bigger current, a stronger magnet or more turns of wire on the coil. Each one makes the turning force stronger.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you make a motor more powerful?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How do you make a motor spin the other way?",
      "a": "Swap the battery connections. The current through the coil reverses, the forces flip, and the motor turns the opposite way.",
      "asks": [
        "How do you reverse a motor?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Michael Faraday demonstrated the first electric motor in 1821.",
    "In simple motors, carbon brushes press on the commutator to carry current into the spinning coil."
  ],
